#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124216 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "69da2e7c-7ede-5966-8ff9-13bca51c0420" :question/math-academy-id "e-4813" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,3] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and its subset $A = \\big\\{f \\in \\mathcal{C}[-1,3] \\: \\mid \\: f(0)=-f(2) \\big\\}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is closed under function addition\n2. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$\n4. $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathcal{C}[-1,3]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x) \\in A$, then\n$(f + g)(0)|= f(0) + g(0); =-f(2) - g(2); = - (f(2) + g(2)); = - (f + g)(2)$,\nwhich means $f+g \\in A$. So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n- Statement II is true. If $f(x) \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$, then\n$(kf)(0)|= kf(0); = k \\cdot (-f(2)); =-kf(2); = - (kf)(2)$,\nwhich means $kf \\in A$. So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement III is true. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124319 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "7fed39de-7304-52a0-a94d-e55703175d7d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8233" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$, and its subset $N$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $N$ is closed under matrix addition\n2. $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $N$ is a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n4. $N$ is **not** a subspace of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $A_1$ and $A_2$ are two matrices from $N$, then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal for each matrix is greater or equal to $0$. Therefore, $A_1+A_2$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater or equal to $0$. This matrix belongs to $N$, and so $N$ is closed under addition.\n- Statement II is false. If $A$ is an element of $N$, then the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is greater than or equal to $0$. If we let $k=-1 \\in \\mathbb{R}$, then $kA$ is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is less than or equal to $0$. This matrix does not belong to $N$, and so $N$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $N$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124320 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "05151ae0-102c-5ce8-8bc6-082e22aa77d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8234" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\mathbb R_3[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3$, and its subset $A = \\big\\{at^3-bt \\: \\mid \\: a,b \\in\\mathbb{R} \\big\\}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n2. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $A$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$\n4. $A$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb R_3[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)=a_1t^3-b_1t$ and $g(t)=a_2t^3-b_2t$ are two polynomials from $A$, then\n$f(t) + g(t)|= (a_{1}t^{3} - b_{1}t) + (a_{2}t^{3} - b_{2}t); = (a_{1} + a_{2})t^{3} - (b_{1} + b_{2})t$,\nwhich is a polynomial from $A$. So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=at^3-bt \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$, then\n$kf(t)|= k(at^{3} - bt); = (ka)t^{3} - (kb)t$,\nwhich is a polynomial from $A$. So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n- Statement III is true. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0c56ca0-5fde-578e-8a11-320867e3c29c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123437} {:answer/id #uuid "e9fdce44-4d82-55cd-b8b7-f25b7d875424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123438} {:answer/id #uuid "0ad7f7ca-c6b6-5bf7-bd00-cb9d3205e75c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123439} {:answer/id #uuid "71368ba5-9984-5976-8c58-be6f5299b2c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123440} {:answer/id #uuid "7bee109f-1897-5140-ad22-59df9ebc77e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bee109f-1897-5140-ad22-59df9ebc77e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123441} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2599b0e-7655-54a7-a465-3b7273682fa8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123436}] :question/id #uuid "f4e607a0-6afc-5179-8279-85636fe2408c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43769" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $R_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2$, and its subset $B = {t^{2} + at + b \\mid a, b ∈ R}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n2. $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n3. $B$ is a subspace of $R_{2}[t]$\n4. $B$ is a **not** a subspace of $R_{2}[t]$"}]}
